Memory layout
=============
Many (all?) of these arrays could be allocated on the heap
Can we then dispose of them at runtime?
Requires more sophisticated heap that can reuse disposed allocations.
Probably want this for sensible array/dict/string behaviour anyway.
Ref-counted objects?


Unaccounted for:

    Program text as entered by user
    Machine stack

41 bytes for simple vars
448 bytes for arrays

Program text entered by user
    prog_base                               // 2 bytes

Interned identifiers, compile - pointers into heap
    ident_bucket[32]                        // 64 bytes

Pre-allocated strings (pointers into heap)
    String* interned_string_empty           // 2 bytes
    String* interned_string_true            // 2 bytes
    String* interned_string_false           // 2 bytes
    String* interned_string_array           // 2 bytes
    String* interned_string_dict            // 2 bytes
    String* interned_string_proc            // 2 bytes
    String* interned_string_func            // 2 bytes
    String* interned_string_unknown         // 2 bytes

Interned strings
    String* string_bucket[256]              // 512 bytes

Heap containing (compile) idents, (compile+runtime) strings, (runtime) dicts, (runtime) arrays
    heap_top                                // 2 bytes
    heap_end                                // 2 bytes

Generated code
    code_base                               // 2 bytes
    code_ptr                                // 2 bytes

Keyword Lookup
    OpData kw                               // 2 bytes

Lexing
    input_ptr                               // 2 bytes
    token_ptr                               // 2 bytes

Parsing
    pending_ops[32]                         // 64 bytes
    pending_ops_sp                          // 2 bytes

Statement Parsing
    control_sp                              // 2 bytes
    control_stack[32]                       // 32 bytes
    begin_loop_sp                           // 2 bytes
    begin_loop_stack[32]                    // 64 bytes
    end_loop_sp                             // 2 bytes
    end_loop_stack[32]                      // 32 bytes
    end_loop_count                          // 2 bytes
    forward_jump_sp                         // 2 bytes
    forward_jump_stack[32]                  // 64 bytes
    func_kind                               // 1 byte
    func_ident                              // 2 bytes
    slot_stack[64]                          // 128 bytes

Virtual Machine
    Value vm_a                              // 3 bytes
    Value vm_b                              // 3 bytes
    u16 vm_fp                               // 2 bytes
    u16 vm_sp                               // 2 bytes
    u16 vm_pc                               // 2 bytes
    vm_stack[1536]                          // 1536 bytes

